esp32s31 GDMA: let the LCD scanout ring allocate with reclaim (fixes a black
screen behind a running game)

Files (whole files): drivers/dma/esp32s31-axi-gdma.c,
include/linux/dma/esp32s31-axi-gdma.h, drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/esp32s31-lcd.c

SYMPTOM (2026-09-21): TyrQuake fullscreen with a 12 MB heap - panel BLACK,
audio still playing, game still running. dmesg:
    [drm] *ERROR* failed to start scanout DMA: -12
The display mode switch re-prepares the cyclic scanout ring, and every GDMA
descriptor allocation was GFP_NOWAIT, which fails outright when memory is
tight rather than reclaiming. Quake had ~1.3 MB MemAvailable.

FIX: a per-channel opt-in, esp32s31_axi_gdma_set_may_sleep(chan, true). Only
the LCD channel sets it; its prep then allocates GFP_KERNEL. Every other GDMA
user keeps GFP_NOWAIT, correct for atomic callers. Verified there is exactly
ONE prep site on that channel (esp32s31_lcd_start_scanout) and ONE caller
(esp32s31_lcd_pipe_enable, the DRM plane-enable callback: process context
under the modeset mutexes, no spinlock on the path). Also: a log line naming
SRAM-descriptor-pool exhaustion, the one case GFP flags cannot fix.

VERIFIED: same scenario after the fix - picture on screen through the second
mode switch, gameplay rendering, 0 "failed to start scanout" in dmesg.

DOWNSIDES, stated: a fullscreen switch under memory pressure may now stall for
reclaim (once, at the switch, never per frame); in the extreme GFP_KERNEL can
invoke the OOM killer instead of failing; the SRAM pool can still run out
(now loudly).

NOTE: an intermediate kernel (#334) panicked at boot. That was NOT this change -
a stale fastarm.sh experiment (irq-esp32s31-clic.o in .text..fast) was still in
the tree's linker script; the panic was the known illegal instruction at
riscv_intc_irq_unmask+0x28, badaddr bfc51047. Restored the committed script.
